        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Same image, rotated 180°.",
          "rect": [
            80,
            420,
            800,
            40
          ],
          "size": 28,
          "color": "#C00000"
        }
      ],
      "speech": "Peekaboo optimized a mask through a frozen diffusion model. Same loss here, new question: what if we optimize several images at once, and they have to share pixels? Look at the giraffe. Turn it upside down, and it's a penguin. This dog, upside down, is an elephant. Nobody trained a model for this. It's frozen Stable Diffusion, and SDXL for the quantitative runs."
    },
    {
      "title": "One print, two pictures",
      "elements": [
        {
          "kind": "image",
          "path": "/Users/rburgert/Documents/ThesisProposal/presentation/agents/.scratchpad/explain_diffillusions/crop_flip_photo.png",
          "rect": [
            100,
            100,
            760,
            401.9
          ]
        }
      ],
      "speech": "Here's one we printed. Right side up, a dog. Rotate the paper, a sloth. The red words are just handwritten labels. The paper is the prime image, the thing you hold. Each way of looking at it is an arrangement, a fixed function that's differentiable and physically doable. What you see is a derived image, and each one gets its own prompt. One prime, two arrangements, identity and rotate 180."
    },
    {
      "title": "But...how?",
      "elements": [
        {
          "kind": "image",
          "path": "/Users/rburgert/Documents/ThesisProposal/presentation/agents/.scratchpad/explain_diffillusions/arch.png",
          "rect": [
            30,
            92,
            900,
            372.6
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Every view gets a loss; all gradients land on the shared primes.",
          "rect": [
            40,
            480,
            880,
            40
          ],
          "size": 24,
          "color": "#C00000"
        }
      ],
      "speech": "But...how? Here's the whole pipeline. Each prime is a small Fourier feature network, an MLP from pixel coordinates to color. The arrangements turn primes into derived images. On each derived image we run score distillation with its prompt: encode, add noise, the frozen model predicts the noise, and the error is a gradient on that view. No backprop through the U-Net, only through the encoder, the arrangements and the MLPs, so every view's gradient sums onto the shared primes. Why an MLP? With raw pixels, the second picture hid in high-frequency noise, and that dies the moment you print."
    },
    {
      "title": "Stack them",
      "elements": [
        {
          "kind": "image",
          "path": "/Users/rburgert/Documents/ThesisProposal/presentation/agents/.scratchpad/explain_diffillusions/crop_rotation_overlay.png",
          "rect": [
            190,
            88,
            580,
            335.1
          ]
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Light through two sheets = multiply the colors",
          "rect": [
            80,
            436,
            800,
            36
          ],
          "size": 26,
          "color": "#C00000"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "d_j = tanh(2 · p1 · rot(p2, 90°·j)),  j = 0, 1, 2, 3",
          "rect": [
            80,
            482,
            800,
            34
          ],
          "size": 22
        }
      ],
      "speech": "What else can an arrangement be? Print two images on transparencies over a backlight. The base stays, the rotator spins on top. Light goes through both, so the color you see is the product. Order doesn't matter, multiplication commutes. Products get dark, so we scale by two and squash with a tanh, like a brighter backlight. Two primes, four rotations, four derived images: cat, man, woman, dog. These are photos of the real prints."
    },
